Fillets: a rounding whose ball rolls on the far edge of a strip narrower
than its radius goes on rolling along that edge's circle past the edge's
vertex wherever the strip's next far edge belongs to a face going on
tangentially from the face beyond the edge, and that face cuts it there,
also where the walk went on rolling on that face's edge or started there
(mitcad#1388).

A fin's side strips, about 0.05 high between the arcs' chamfer cone and
the root's chamfer, are rounded with 0.86 against the root's chamfer. The
ball rolls on the strip's edge with the arc's chamfer (a circle) and
touches the root's chamfer: a torus. At each end of that edge the strip's
far edge goes on as its edge with a transition piece W of the arc's
chamfer, tangent to the cone there, and narrows to the spine's end vertex
V. The stored bodies run the torus on along the circle past the edge's
vertex A and cut it by W; RolledOnProlongedEdgeAtEnd (0904) does so where
the walk past A failed. On a body 0.003 mm off the stored one (the root's
chamfer a spline instead of a cone) the walk instead:

- found its start rolling on W's edge, beside V, and rolled into V at once
  or failed where its other contact crossed onto W: StartSolOverflow now
  takes such a start, a side rolling on an edge that runs into the spine's
  end vertex, only where no other start is found;
- went on past A rolling on W's edge into V: RolledOnProlongedEdgeAtEnd now
  drops the pieces at that end that roll on edges of a face W holding V,
  W going on tangentially (within a tenth of a radian) from the face beyond
  Er at A, or turn about A on Er, and rolls the blend on from the piece
  before them (the dropped pieces generate no face). At an end face that
  meets the cone at an angle (the fin's end, 45 degrees) the walked pieces
  stay, as in the stored bodies;
- at the first end, walked back from P's other end past the spine's end,
  was completed forward over P and the rest of the stripe: that walk is
  not completed forward (ChFi3d_SetStartsAtPieceBefore), as a walk back
  from a piece's start.

On the design's body (its chamfer's spline pieces joined, as MitCad's
dressup does), the far walls and the near wall at x = -0.5 with the fin's
end rim (the eleven edges the stored body rounds) give the stored body's
152 faces, valid, every face within 0.003 mm of the stored one (the body's
own deviation); the near wall alone with either order of its edges, 149
faces within 0.0006 mm. Before, the near wall failed in both orders of
its edges, and so did the eleven edges. On the stored state before the
item, the same 152 faces as before.

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_fin_end_strips.cpp, on the case
`fin-end-strips` of MITCAD_GEOMETRY_CASES.

Upstream: new.

